Temporal and Spatial Stability Analysis of the Orr-Sommerfeld Equation
This is the second and final part of the stability analysis of the Orr-Sommerfeld Equation. In my previous post, I went through the derivation and nondimensionalization of the Orr-Sommerfeld Equation. In this part, I show how to perform the temporal and spatial stability analyses.


Blasius Velocity Profile
A 2-D Blasius boundary layer can be expressed as

	U¯=f′(η)		(1)

where

η=yU∞2νx
f‴+ff″	=0
f(0)=f′(0)	=0
f′(∞)	=1

This is a nonlinear ordinary differential equation that is most easily solved using a shooting method. In a shooting method, the boundary value problem is made into an initial value problem, whereby an initial guess of one parameter is iteratively updated until the opposite boundary conditions are met. In the present case, the unknown boundary condition is f″(0) , so it is iteratively adjusted until |f′(10)−1|≤10−6. The present case uses a fourth-order Runge-Kutta method of integration. The initial condition The transformation of the Blasius velocity profile from η to ξ is performed by determining the distance from the wall, δ, where the stream-wise velocity is 99.9% of the free-stream velocity.

	U¯(ξ)	=U¯(ηδ)
U¯″(ξ)	=1δ2U¯″(ηδ)		(2)

The figure below shows the Blasius velocity profile and its derivatives.




Temporal Stability
The Orr-Sommerfeld equation can be analyzed for temporal stability by assuming that α¯ is real. This enables us to rearrange the non-dimensional Orr-Sommerfeld equation as follows.
	(−U¯α¯2−U″+iα¯3Reδ)ϕ¯+(U¯−2iα¯Reδ)ϕ¯″+(iα¯Reδ)ϕ¯⁗=c¯(ϕ¯″−α¯2ϕ¯)		(3)
If we discretize this equation using a finite difference approximation for ϕ¯″ and ϕ¯⁗, we get an equation of the form
	AΦ=c~BΦ		(4)
where Φ is a vector containing values ϕ¯i at discrete locations. This is nothing more than an eigenvalue problem, where c~ is a diagonal matrix of eigenvalues for the discrete system. The present calculations use a second order central differencing scheme to approximate the second and fourth derivative terms.
	ϕ¯i″	≈ϕ¯i−1−2ϕ¯i+ϕ¯i+1h2
ϕ¯i⁗	≈ϕ¯i−2−4ϕ¯i−1+6ϕ¯i−4ϕ¯i+1+ϕ¯i+2h4		(5)
At the boundaries, ϕ¯=0, so the first and last columns of A and B are removed. However, ϕ¯′=0 also at the boundaries. Fortunately, using a backward difference method at the boundary gives ϕ¯−1≈ϕ¯0, so the ϕ¯i−2 term can be omitted from the ϕ¯⁗ approximation just inside the wall boundary. Similarly, the ϕ¯i+2 term can be omitted just inside the free-stream boundary.
The finite difference approximations in Equation 5 gives a pentadiagonal A and tridiagonal B.
